 "text": "ITUPublications\n\n##### Measuring digital development\n\n### Facts and Figures\n\n2026\n\nInternational Telecommunication Union Development Sector\n\nPlease consider the environment before printing this report.\n\n© ITU 2026 Some rights reserved. This work is licensed to the public through a Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-Share Alike 3.0 IGO license (CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO). Under the terms of this licence, you may copy, redistribute and adapt the work for non-commercial purposes, provided the work is appropriately cited. In any use of this work, there should be no suggestion that ITU endorse any specific organization, products or services. The unauthorized use of the ITU names or logos is not permitted. If you adapt the work, then you must license your work under the same or equivalent Creative Commons licence. If you create a translation of this work, you should add the following disclaimer along with the suggested citation: “This translation was not created by the International Telecommunication Union (ITU). ITU is not responsible for the content or accuracy of this translation. The original English edition shall be the binding and authentic edition”. For more information, please visit https://creativecommons.org/ licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/igo/\n\nDisclaimers \nThe designations employed and the presentation of the material in this publication do not imply \nthe expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of the International Telecommunication \nUnion (ITU) or of the ITU secretariat concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city, or \narea or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. \nThe mention of specific companies or of certain manufacturers’ products does not imply that they \nare endorsed or recommended by ITU in preference to others of a similar nature that are not \nmentioned. Errors and omissions excepted; the names of proprietary products are distinguished \nby initial capital letters. \nAll reasonable precautions have been taken by ITU to verify the information contained in this \npublication. However, the published material is being distributed without warranty of any kind, \neither expressed or implied. The responsibility for the interpretation and use of the material lies \nwith the reader. \nThe opinions, findings and conclusions expressed in this publication do not necessarily reflect \nthe views of ITU or its membership. \n\nISBN 978-92-61-43571-4 (Electronic version) 978-92-61-43581-3 (EPUB version)\n\n#### Measuring digital development\n\n## Facts and Figures 2026\n\niv\n\nForeword\n\nThe 2026 edition of Facts and Figures offers a fresh snapshot of global digital connectivity, highlighting both the progress achieved and the challenges that remain on the path towards universal and meaningful connectivity. This year marks an important milestone: more than three-quarters of the world’s population are now online. An estimated 6.3 billion people use the Internet, up from 6.1 billion in 2025. Yet 2 billion people remain offline, reminding us that the benefits of digital connectivity are still far from universal. Behind the global figures, significant divides persist. Internet use stands at 93 per cent in high income countries, compared with just 32 per cent in low-income countries. Women remain less likely than men to use the Internet, and substantial gaps persist between urban and rural populations. Although Internet use is growing relatively rapidly in least developed countries and landlocked developing countries, these countries continue to face considerable challenges. There are also encouraging developments. 5G now covers almost six in ten people worldwide, while 97 per cent of the global population has access to a 3G or more advanced mobile network. Broadband services are also becoming more affordable. Notably, landlocked developing countries and Small Island Developing States have, for the first time, met the 2 per cent affordability target for data-only mobile broadband. These advances are essential, but connectivity is not an end in itself. It opens up opportunities in education, healthcare and economic development, and provides the backbone for emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence. Yet without adequate connectivity and digital skills, the benefits of these technologies risk remaining out of reach for many, reinforcing existing digital divides. The findings also highlight the importance of better data. Reliable, timely and disaggregated statistics are essential for understanding digital divides and shaping effective policies. Yet fewer than half of countries have reported recent official data on Internet use, with even lower coverage for digital skills and mobile phone ownership. I therefore call on ITU Member States to strengthen the collection and reporting of ICT statistics, particularly through regular household surveys and timely, disaggregated data. The progress recorded in 2026 gives us reason for optimism, but also a clear call to action. We must accelerate our collective efforts to close the remaining digital divides and ensure that everyone, everywhere can benefit from meaningful connectivity.\n\nDr Cosmas Luckyson Zavazava Director of the Telecommunication Development Bureau International Telecommunication Union\n\nv\n\nForeword �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������iv \nInternet use�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 1 \nThe gender digital divide�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 3 \nYouth Internet use �������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 6 \nInternet use in urban and rural areas����������������������������